WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.180
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.270
of "VM End-to-End," a show
where we have a VM skeptic

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.580
and a VM enthusiast get
together and hash out

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.380
all things VM related.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.340
Brian, thanks again for coming
in and spitting knowledge

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:18.840
at me.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.840
BRIAN DORSEY: I'm so
excited about this one.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.177
Disks, disks, disks.

00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:23.510
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, you would--

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:24.500
I don't understand this.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.958
I've been trying to figure this
out for three episodes now.

00:00:26.958 --> 00:00:29.305
What is it about disks that
is so exciting for you?

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:30.680
BRIAN DORSEY: I
think part of it,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.120
for me, is that I think
almost all useful systems have

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.250
to store their information
somewhere, right?

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:42.110
Your system has a memory,
and you care about the data.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.270
Most of the time, that's
on a disk somewhere,

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.980
or ends up on a disk in the end.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.710
And so I think, for me,
it's where systems get real.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.030
And--

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.170
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.087
BRIAN DORSEY:
--that's the big deal.

00:00:56.087 --> 00:00:57.420
CARTER MORGAN: I could see that.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.320
It's a fundamental
unit of computing.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.570
And so that's what's exciting.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.362
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:01:01.362 --> 00:01:03.450
And on that unit,
we think of them

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.355
as physical hard
disks or maybe an SSD.

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:08.730
But let me turn
this back on you.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.020
In the cloud, in Compute Engine,
what do you think a disk is?

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:13.770
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.280
All right.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.260
Going back to a slice of a
data-center model, to me,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.097
a disk would be
a bunch of pieces

00:01:20.097 --> 00:01:21.680
from real physical
machines spread out

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.690
over a data warehouse
or a data center,

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.000
pulled together, and
presented to me, the user,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.910
as one logical disk that I
can work with and operate on.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.320
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.540
Spot-on.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:33.830
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.770
BRIAN DORSEY: That
is exactly it.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:42.150
And in fact, some of that-- so
it's a network-attached disk,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:42.650
right?

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.480
And it speaks of a protocol.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.480
And even physical disks, these
days, speak of packet protocol.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.648
So SATA is serial ATA.

00:01:49.648 --> 00:01:50.940
And they talk about the blocks.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.315
So here's a block of data, or
give me that block of data.

00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:57.980
And ours speak of similar
protocol, but over the network,

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.010
to a group of disks.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.710
CARTER MORGAN: So
I guess, what is

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.960
it that's exciting about
that, and especially

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.280
the network-attached part,
because what I care about

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.570
is performance.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.610
I care about backups
and scalability.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.360
I care about reliability more
so than a fundamental unit

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.290
of computing.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.990
Where do these disks sit
in relation to those?

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:20.800
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:21.300
OK.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.990
This is a meaty question.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:27.400
So I think, actually, let's talk
about each of these in a row.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.650
So performance, to start.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.270
I think performance is tricky.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.610
What does it mean to be fast?

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.400
And I think there's actually
two different parts of that,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.000
for disks.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.660
One is throughput.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.960
In a bulk, how much
data can you copy over

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.280
a certain amount of time?

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.330
And the other is latency.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.120
So how quickly can you
get a response back

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.875
to a specific question?

00:02:51.875 --> 00:02:52.750
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.110
It feels like a
physical disk is going

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.810
to be faster than that because
I don't have to communicate over

00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.860
a network.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.450
That seems like
common sense to me.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.700
BRIAN DORSEY: And I agree
it seems like common sense.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.920
And it's true for one
and not for the other.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.800
And it's weird why that is.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.115
So as physical disks are
getting faster over time--

00:03:15.115 --> 00:03:16.990
sorry, as they're getting
larger and faster--

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.150
but they're getting
larger faster

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.810
than they're getting faster.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.620
So there's more and more
capacity on the disk.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.930
And the window to
read and write that

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.860
is not getting as big as fast.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.760
So if you're talking
over the network

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.400
to hundreds or
thousands of disks,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.130
you can actually get
more overall throughput.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.620
So the throughput
side is an area

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.940
where we are actually faster
than a single physical disk.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.260
But sometimes-- there is
a little extra step there.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.327
So if you need the absolute
lowest latency read,

00:03:49.327 --> 00:03:50.660
you might need something faster.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.322
So we have a product,
local SSD, to do that.

00:03:54.322 --> 00:03:55.780
CARTER MORGAN: And
that's basically

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.953
just literally attaching a very
fast hard drive to a machine.

00:03:59.953 --> 00:04:01.120
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, it's a--

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:01.870
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.760
BRIAN DORSEY: --physical
SSD on the host

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.500
that we are running your VM on.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.250
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.822
So that seems, to me, like--

00:04:09.822 --> 00:04:11.280
I come from video
game programming.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.290
So is it something I
would use for maybe

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:14.790
an inventory-management system?

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.703
Something like, I recall
items in inventory often.

00:04:17.703 --> 00:04:18.870
Is that a good fit for this?

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.660
BRIAN DORSEY: It would seem like
it, but I think, in this case,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.290
probably not.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:27.180
So the local SSDs are actually
intended for temporary storage

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.580
because they are mapped
to this physical host.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.050
And we want our VMs to be
long-lived and able to move

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:34.710
around.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.790
So there are cases
where we can't

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.850
have the data live as long as
you might like on a local SSD.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.930
So they're best fit for
things like caches or data

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.710
that can be regenerated by
processing or transcoding

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.140
workloads or things like that.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.180
CARTER MORGAN:
Intermediate calculations

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.600
in math or vectors or whatnot.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.100
OK.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.170
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:57.330
CARTER MORGAN: All right.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.663
That makes a lot of sense, then.

00:04:58.663 --> 00:05:02.940
So then what does
Compute Engine offer

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.670
when it comes to different
types of physical disks

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.770
or different types of
PDs, persistent disks?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.150
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.880
I think there are
multiple different types,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.130
and I'm probably not going
to go into all the details.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.010
But the basic
breakdown is an area

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.988
that I think you
didn't hit here that I

00:05:18.988 --> 00:05:20.280
think does matter to everybody.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.170
And that's cost.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.670
CARTER MORGAN: Oh,
that's a big one.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.390
Yeah.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.300
BRIAN DORSEY: So it
basically boils down

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.680
to a price-to-performance ratio.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.100
So when you have a
workload that is really

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.090
performance-sensitive, there
are versions of persistent disks

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:37.147
that can hit that.

00:05:37.147 --> 00:05:38.980
And they tend to be a
little more expensive.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.700
And if you've got a
workload that is mostly

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.490
about storing information, and
you don't access it that often,

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:49.630
there are less expensive
modes of PD to do that.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.663
CARTER MORGAN: And
that makes sense to me.

00:05:51.663 --> 00:05:53.080
If I was a
small-business owner, I

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.030
might not need the
speed and scale

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.730
that, say, a company
like the size of Google

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:58.708
would operate at.

00:05:58.708 --> 00:05:59.500
BRIAN DORSEY: And--

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.100
CARTER MORGAN: So then--

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:00.460
BRIAN DORSEY: Sorry, I
just cut you off there.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.390
CARTER MORGAN: --to
not get into-- oh--

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.290
BRIAN DORSEY: But--

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.280
CARTER MORGAN: Mm-hmm.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.220
BRIAN DORSEY: --to that point,
I think lots of workloads need

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:06.940
both as--

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.140
or, systems need both.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:11.140
You've got a long-term storage
and a high-speed portion

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.710
as well.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:13.460
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.210
That's actually a
really good point.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.240
So then, OK, talking
about systems--

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.940
and like you said, in
systems in the while

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.740
might need two types of disk.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.050
What about backing up
these kind of systems

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.550
that are in production?

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:29.090
Do these virtual-network
disks actually

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.587
help with reliability
or backing up data?

00:06:31.587 --> 00:06:32.420
BRIAN DORSEY: A lot.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:33.020
Absolutely.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:39.120
So like you said, it's this
logical collection of blocks,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:39.620
right?

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:43.460
So in many ways, I
think it's like having

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.800
most of a SAN, a
storage area network.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.880
So you get most of the features
you would want from that.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.410
And by that, I mean you can
take a snapshot of a disk, which

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.830
makes a point-in-time copy
without slowing the disk down.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:02.850
So you can make backups of
live systems any time you want.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.452
And the other thing
you can do is,

00:07:04.452 --> 00:07:06.410
you can take an existing
disk, and you can just

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.750
make it larger, even while
it's connected to a machine.

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:10.897
I feel like everybody
has run out of disk space

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:11.480
at some point.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.327
CARTER MORGAN: I have a laptop,
and it's run out of space.

00:07:14.327 --> 00:07:16.160
And there's nothing I
can really do about it

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.050
other than plugging in
an extra hard drive.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:21.080
So that's a feature that
makes a lot of sense to me.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.920
You said something earlier about
snapshots and whatnot, though.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.370
And I'm curious, what's
exciting about that?

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.790
How does that become usable
in a production system?

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:37.230
BRIAN DORSEY: So there's the
straight-up backup use case,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.440
which I think is important.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:45.010
But then one of the big ways we
scale computers these days is--

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.010
there's vertical, and
there's horizontal, right?

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.475
So vertical might be like
making the disk bigger,

00:07:49.475 --> 00:07:50.350
like we talked about.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.850
And then horizontal is, add more
computers to the mix doing it.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:57.510
And so if you have
a computer that's

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.070
set up the way you
want it to work,

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.980
you can take a snapshot of that.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.500
And then you can
make multiple images

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.630
of that that are running
on multiple machines

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:10.000
and have them work as
a group to do the work.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:10.750
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.220
That is actually really
cool, because you're

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.368
going to get reliability
and consistency there.

00:08:14.368 --> 00:08:15.910
You're not going to
get human errors.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:16.702
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:08:16.702 --> 00:08:19.720
CARTER MORGAN: I am
a huge fan of that.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.180
Is there anything else there
that you want to touch on?

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.940
Because, like you said, you're
going to get backups from this.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.550
You're going to be able to scale
vertically and horizontally

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:28.990
from this.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.679
I feel like those are some
really big benefits to disks

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:35.860
and why they're
useful, and even more

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:41.855
useful than having a physical
non-cloud-native disk.

00:08:41.855 --> 00:08:43.980
So what do we need to follow
up with in the future?

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:44.970
We said cost.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:45.250
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.667
CARTER MORGAN: I missed cost,
and that's really important.

00:08:47.667 --> 00:08:49.501
So let's definitely
have an episode on cost.

00:08:49.501 --> 00:08:50.520
What else am I missing?

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:51.810
BRIAN DORSEY: I think a whole
episode there makes sense.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.480
And then, I think,
with the scaling stuff,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:55.950
you get more moving
parts, right?

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.982
So if you go horizontal,
you'll have more computers.

00:08:58.982 --> 00:08:59.940
You'll have more disks.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:03.010
You start-- need to think
about networking patterns.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.400
And so it's useful to
have some automation

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.888
to set that up and configure
it in a consistent way.

00:09:08.888 --> 00:09:10.430
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah,
and I'm actually

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.980
excited about that because
I'm a big fan of automating

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:13.940
as much as I can.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:15.020
I make mistakes.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.090
I don't want to risk it.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.830
Brian, thanks again for
just taking the time.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.400
I've got to admit,
slowly, you're

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:23.690
winning me over, because--

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:24.607
BRIAN DORSEY: Love it.

00:09:24.607 --> 00:09:28.640
CARTER MORGAN: --there seems
to be a lot of benefits.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:29.300
Thank you.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.020
That's all for us this episode.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.397
If you're watching this at
home, let me know anything

00:09:33.397 --> 00:09:35.480
that you think we missed
talking about or anything

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:36.858
that you thought was very cool.

00:09:36.858 --> 00:09:38.150
I'm getting won over right now.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:39.067
Are you with me or no?

00:09:39.067 --> 00:09:40.670
Let us know in the
comments below.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:44.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

